      facts: ["Recommended Age", "Difficulty", "Estimated Play Time", "Skills Trained"], values: ["9+", "Easy to Challenging", "1–3 minutes per stage", "Logic · Reading · Problem Solving"],
      overview: "Lantern Lattice is a quiet logic puzzle set in a moonlit meadow. Orla, the lantern keeper, leaves relationship clues that connect six animal lanterns into a safe night route. Each path has a definite order. Read the clues, build that order with the lantern buttons, and check the finished chain. This is a thoughtful puzzle rather than a speed test: there is no countdown, life meter, opponent, or penalty that forces a quick guess. Pause to compare a clue with your choices, clear the chain, and try again. The challenge comes from interpreting relationships and noticing how each new rule changes the route.",
      stepIntro: "Start a campaign to follow the arcs in order, or open Stages to choose an unlocked path. A path may contain three to six lantern positions.",
      steps: ["Read every keeper clue before choosing a lantern. The first clue establishes a starting light; later clues describe what follows it.", "Tap the lanterns in order. Your growing chain stays visible so you can compare it with the clues.", "Use Check path when the chain is complete. An incomplete chain tells you how many choices remain; a wrong chain names the first misplaced link or the moth decoy.", "Use Reset chain to clear your choices and retry the same path. Wrong checks count toward that path’s best-check record, but never remove lives or unlocked progress.", "A clear lights the next path and records your progress in this browser. Use Next Stage to enter the next Battle directly, Stages to choose a route, or Replay to try the settled route again.", "Use shared Settings to change the language or sound. The return arrow leads back to the previous screen; the puzzle remains playable with sound off."],
      rules: "The first arc teaches direct order: follow each relationship from the nest toward the gate. A moth-decoy path adds one tempting lantern that must stay dark and outside your chain. Echo paths repeat one named lantern in two positions; that second appearance is part of the answer. Wind-turn paths reverse the reading direction, so begin at the last lantern and follow the clues back to the first. The later arcs combine these ideas, sometimes asking you to read backward, place an echo twice, and leave a decoy unlit in the same route. Clues stay visible while you build the chain. An incomplete chain asks for more choices; a complete but incorrect chain points to the earliest mismatch so you know where to reread.",
      progression: "The journey contains 30 authored paths in six connected arcs, with checkpoints at paths 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, and 30. Paths 1–5 introduce the animal lights and direct relationships. Paths 6–10 add moth decoys; 11–15 introduce echoes; 16–20 focus on wind-turn reversal; 21–25 combine echoes with decoys; and 26–30 bring the rules together for mastery. Completing a path unlocks the next one and records its clear. The Stages map keeps unlocked routes available, so replaying a short path never erases later progress. Checkpoint badges mark each arc’s end, while the core action—read, connect, check—stays familiar.",
      tips: "Read the whole clue list once before tapping, then translate each sentence into a relationship such as “A comes before B.” Solve one link at a time instead of guessing a full order from a single clue. On a wind-turn path, first work out the ordinary order and then reverse it; separating the clue logic from direction reduces mistakes. On an echo path, reserve the named lantern for both positions described by the clues. Keep the moth decoy out of the chain even if it looks like a likely answer. When a check names a link, revisit the clue that governs that position before resetting. The game has no timer, so careful comparison is useful.",
      design: "The board keeps its clues beside the lantern choices so observation and deduction remain in one place. Six distinct animal lights and a numbered chain make the current route easy to inspect. Feedback names the first incorrect link instead of hiding the puzzle behind a generic failure message. Each arc introduces a recognizable rule, then later paths combine familiar rules; this gives the campaign a gradual shape without changing its basic controls. The moonlit meadow is a fictional puzzle setting, not a claim about real animal behavior. The pace is deliberately calm, and optional sound can be muted without losing information needed to solve a path.",
      saved: "Use touch, mouse, or keyboard focus with Enter or Space to operate buttons. Shared Settings provides language and sound controls on Main and play screens. Cleared paths, unlocked progress, and best check counts are stored in this browser when local storage is available. The record belongs to this browser on this device; it does not sync to an account or move automatically to another device. Clearing site data, private browsing, or blocked storage can remove or limit that local record. If storage is unavailable, you can still play, but the browser may not retain progress after the page closes.",
